105: ## Interpretation
106: 
107: - `makespan` is logical simulated ticks to final successful family commit, not
108:   wall time. Deferred/non-selected units prevent claiming mission completion.
109: - `usefulWorkerTicks` counts only completed, fixture-published families' final
110:   attempts. Failed/fenced attempts and abandoned completed roles are rework.
111: - `utilization = usefulWorkerTicks / (workerCount * makespan)`. Reservations,
112:   validation and failed work do not inflate useful utilization.
113: - Metrics version 2 distinguishes nominal role work from occupied execution time.
114:   One tick is a logical integer simulation tick; speed scales each execution as
115:   `max(1, ceil(baseRoleTicks / workerSpeed))`. Useful/rework worker ticks retain
116:   these scheduled execution ticks, excluding provider-response latency. They are
117:   not monetary cost. `occupiedWorkerTicks` sums actual execution hold intervals
118:   (including stopped/failed work and late latency), without counting gaps between
119:   family jobs or worker-free validation as occupancy.
120: - `publicationEndTick` is the last successful commit, `occupationEndTick` the
121:   last actual execution release (or observation tick for still-held jobs), and
122:   `observationEndTick` the processed event frontier, including lease housekeeping.
123:   `occupationHorizonTicks=max(publicationEndTick, occupationEndTick)` and
124:   `occupationCapacityTicks=workerCount*occupationHorizonTicks` define
125:   `occupationUtilization`. A late held execution after the last publication
126:   remains counted. This denominator is intentionally different from useful
127:   utilization's historical `totalWorkerTicks=workerCount*makespan`.
128: - Speedup is the sequential-completion makespan divided by policy completion
129:   makespan. It is null for incomplete simulations. This is **not native speedup**.
130: - `criticalPathTicks` is the nominal unit-speed longest path through the complete
131:   selected sub-intent DAG, G1 gates and one validation sink per family, with
132:   unlimited compatible workers and no contention/incidents. Recursive members
133:   chain previous R -> next A/B; nonrecursive local calls preserve their real
134:   waits. It is not actual elapsed time, nor the policy rank or a serialized-role
135:   schedule. `totalWorkTicks` is the sum of base role work, excluding validation.
136: - `lowerBoundTicks` is the maximum of that DAG recomputed with per-task optimistic
137:   `max(1,ceil(base/max(1,maximumWorkerSpeed)))` durations and nominal total work
138:   divided by summed worker speeds. Validation is never accelerated. Constraints,
139:   serialization and response latency can loosen, not strengthen, this minorant.
140:   `estimatedRemainingCriticalPathTicks` recomputes the actual task/validation
141:   residue: completed work is zero, running work uses nominal unelapsed work,
142:   pending work remains. It excludes unpredictable incident latency/replanning and
143:   is not a claim that blocked/stale work can complete under the current export.
144: - Waits sum mutually classified family-level intervals, not disjoint worker
145:   idle time. They can exceed makespan. `revisionLagSum` sums global commit
146:   revision lag at dispatch; unrelated commits count as lag, not invalidation.
147:   Stale active-context events and frozen attempt counts are separately reported.
148: - Wait snapshots add each still-open interval at `observationEndTick` to closed
149:   counters, both by reason and per unit. Two units waiting eight ticks contribute
150:   sixteen unit-ticks. Observing never closes or accumulates the interval in runtime
151:   state; a real cancellation/fence/terminal transition closes the relevant wait.
152:   This is separate from continued execution occupancy after a fence.
153: - `SimEngine.observe()` returns a version-1, recursively frozen, detached
154:   `SimObservation` DTO with copied metrics, units, trace, decisions, bulletins and
155:   diagnostics. It contains **no live SimStore** or store mutation methods. Repeated
156:   observation and mutation of a caller-owned clone do not affect continuation;
157:   an earlier observation remains unchanged after later events. `run()` preserves
158:   its existing result API. `runUntil(t)` processes events through `t`; absent an
159:   event there, it does not invent elapsed observation time at `t`.
160: - Wall/CPU and endpoint RSS/heap deltas are measured. RSS is process-wide,
161:   influenced by previous policies/GC, **not per-policy peak allocation**.
162:   Runs are single-threaded and environment/runtime versions are emitted.
163: - FIFO global READY is already bottom-up event-driven. These two labels have
164:   identical decisions here; no fictitious performance difference is reported.
165: 
166: ## Complexity and explicit boundaries
167: 
168: Generator construction is O(N*degree+E+local intents/edges); conflict keys are
169: incidences, not all pairs. Historical `constructionSteps` counts graph operations
170: only; `localDagConstructionSteps` and `totalConstructionSteps` explicitly expose
171: the added local-DAG work. These operation counts are not wall/CPU time. Temporal
172: metrics walk the local/G1 DAG, not just its quotient. Events/READY use heaps;
173: active family/resource ownership excludes historical scans. Resource release
174: coalesces keys in a wake worklist and discards invalid waiters. Each popped live
175: waiter rechecks all of its keys: re-parking on another held key relays the original
176: opportunity. An eligible waiter carries a wake ticket, not ownership; capacity,
177: barrier, pause, cancellation or re-parking returns that ticket, and reservation
178: rechecks actual holders. Resource-ineligible waiters remain READY while the relay
179: continues. This can visit multiple waiters; pops, key and worker probes are counted. READY heap
180: entries are generation-tagged to prevent retries reviving stale queue entries.
181: Local sub-intent completion advances indexed followers.
182: Dispatch scans K resource-combination bucket heads and W worker records:
183: O(K*W) per scan, possibly repeated per admission/event, plus counted wake relay
184: work. Heterogeneous selection scans workers rather than sorting them. Bucket
185: keys contain all requirement dimensions; incompatible heads cannot hide a
186: different compatible resource contract. The bounded tests measure K=1 and
187: K=N for N=16/32/64, as well as the existing 10k sparse case: **no general
188: near-linear claim for K≈N**.
189: Fan-in checking of arbitrary local waits can be expensive; v1 author/reviewer
190: waits and the tested ring/group sizes are bounded. Counters expose readiness,
191: conflict, compatibility, consumer edge, heap, admission and dispatch work.
192: `resourceBucketScans`, `workerVisits`, `accountCapacityChecks` and
193: `conflictWaiterPops` supplement them; low conflictChecks/readyPops alone say
194: nothing about total K*W cost. Local DAG node/edge counts are also exposed.
195: 
196: `conflictWakeKeyVisits`, `conflictHolderVisits` and `conflictMembershipUpdates`
197: add worklist and holder-accounting costs. Membership tests/updates are average
198: O(1) per key/identity; a dynamic conflict visits its direct key's holders, not a
199: transitive component. Wake relay costs O(popped waiters * (their inspected keys +
200: W)), in addition to the repeated K*W dispatch scans. Memory is proportional to
201: holder/key incidences, waiters and tickets, not all conflict pairs. Repeated
202: releases/re-parks can repeat work; these are not universal linear-time bounds.
203: The new bounded K0/Ki fixture checks at most three pops per two-key waiter plus
204: one for its single-key competitor: initial K0, Ki, then possibly K0 again. Its
205: special topology is explicit and is not a bound for arbitrary graphs/incidents.
206: 
207: `simPlanFromExport` checks L5 plan/sidecar/binding/unit fingerprints and copies
208: sub-intent dependencies and artifact requirements. It does not call L5 at
209: runtime. Fixture publications are immutable, sequential-revision, level 1 only.
210: Level 2 is blocked without fabricating a parent/local publication. Admission
211: envelopes include the simulator wrapper and UTF-8 allocations; they are not
212: claimed byte-identical to the native transport.
213: 
214: Export prerequisite evidence **v2** additionally retains the actual detached
215: MissionPlan document. The reader refuses old exported evidence v1 (no replay
216: backfill); genuinely source-less manual fixtures (including resource/projection
217: v2) retain their historical assumptions. Projection version is not export origin:
218: the current DTO/self-hashes cannot distinguish a true manual input from an object
219: whose entire origin metadata was removed. This total-origin stripping/authenticity
220: limit is not solved here; a supplied invalid source never falls back to manual.
221: Uncited prerequisite provenance remains separately **v1**. Archive fingerprints
222: and namespace joins prove supplied-document coherence, not native authenticity.
223: Boundary validation copies/hashes the document and uses indexed joins proportional
224: to its units, prerequisites and owned/context memberships; canonical serialization
225: also sorts object keys. Detached archive storage/copy cost is proportional to the
226: document. No archive scan/rehash is added to the event loop or observation, and no
227: universal linear-time scheduling or new benchmark result is claimed.
228: 
229: `captureReplayJournal`/`replayScenarioJournal` serialize immutable scenario
230: inputs and compare the replay trace hash. Boundary tests also stop and resume
231: the event queue at each tick. Neither is a SQLite process-crash snapshot proof.
232: Controller races, outbox recovery, heartbeats, leases, stops and late callbacks
233: here are **in-memory incident simulations**. L6 owns real SQLite CAS/outbox/
234: attestation tests. Actual junction/symlink ancestors, native actor/profile
235: readback and ASM/scientific validation remain integration obligations, listed
236: in `SIM_INTEGRATION_LIMITS`. A bulletin may pass its own format/author checks
237: but never becomes canonical facts or changes author contexts/dependencies.
